  "content": {
    "id": "p71-q37-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "37",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p71-q37-definition",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "f(x)=(ax-p)^2+(bx-q)^2",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p71-q37-domain",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Take ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "a,b,p,q,x\\in\\mathbb{R}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p71-q37-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why the graph of $y=f(x)$ can never be below the $x$-axis.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Both squares are nonnegative for real $x$, so $f(x)\\ge0$.",
          "worked": "Both $(ax-p)^2$ and $(bx-q)^2$ are nonnegative for real $x$.\n\nTheir sum is nonnegative, so $f(x)\\ge0$ and the graph cannot lie below the $x$-axis.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p71-q37-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p71-q37-b-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Show that",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p71-q37-b-identity",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "f(x)=(a^2+b^2)x^2-2(ap+bq)x+(p^2+q^2).",
                  "display": false
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$$f(x)=(a^2+b^2)x^2-2(ap+bq)x+(p^2+q^2).$$\n\nExpand both squares and collect like terms.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=(a^2x^2-2apx+p^2)+(b^2x^2-2bqx+q^2)\\\\f(x)&=(a^2+b^2)x^2-(2ap+2bq)x+(p^2+q^2)\\\\f(x)&=(a^2+b^2)x^2-2(ap+bq)x+(p^2+q^2).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p71-q37-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p71-q37-c-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Hence, show that",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p71-q37-c-inequality",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "(ap+bq)^2\\le(a^2+b^2)(p^2+q^2).",
                  "display": false
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$$(ap+bq)^2\\le(a^2+b^2)(p^2+q^2).$$\n\nIf $a^2+b^2>0$, use $f(x)\\ge0$ for all real $x$ and $\\Delta\\le0$. If $a=b=0$, both sides are zero.",
          "worked": "If $a^2+b^2=0$, then $a=b=0$ and both sides of the required inequality are zero.\n\nOtherwise, $f$ is quadratic and $f(x)\\ge0$ for all real $x$, so $\\Delta\\le0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}[-2(ap+bq)]^2-4(a^2+b^2)(p^2+q^2)&\\le0\\\\4(ap+bq)^2-4(a^2+b^2)(p^2+q^2)&\\le0\\\\4(ap+bq)^2&\\le4(a^2+b^2)(p^2+q^2)\\\\(ap+bq)^2&\\le(a^2+b^2)(p^2+q^2).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
